// Title: Suspicious Kernel Dump Using Dtrace
// Sigma ID: 7124aebe-4cd7-4ccb-8df0-6d6b93c96795
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Florian Roth (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2021-12-28 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: discovery, T1082
// Description: Detects suspicious way to dump the kernel on Windows systems using dtrace.exe, which is
//   available on Windows systems since Windows 10 19H1
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://twitter.com/0gtweet/status/1474899714290208777?s=12
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/drivers/devtest/dtrace
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/adf5a626c4a776f3f023056f0f398b9523a2f09e/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_dtrace_kernel_dump.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\dtrace.exe" and ProcessCommandLine contains "lkd(0)") or (ProcessCommandLine contains "syscall:::return" and ProcessCommandLine contains "lkd(")
